# Why a computation did not end normally (`[task.cause]`): an error (`Fail`) or a defect (`Die`).
# An interruption is not a cause: it is never received as a value.
#
# A value of "one of the errors in E" cannot be written (`[error.decl]`), so a `Cause[E]` does not
# give out its error. Look at its kind, show it, or raise it again with `Cause.raise` and catch the
# error with `escape`.
#
# The functions are built in: the runtime makes and reads causes. Lowering turns them into
# concurrency operations of the Core IR, not rows of the builtin table.
#
# `Cause[E]` has a field only to make `E` an error set. Without a field, nothing would decide the
# kind of `E` (`[type.fn]` decides it where it is used), and as an ordinary type parameter `E` of
# `Exit[A, E]` and of `attempt` would be a single error type (`[error.fail]`).

# The runtime holds the value. The field only makes `E` an error set (`[type.fn]`); it is never
# read. It is not named `raise`, the function that raises the cause again (E3109).
## The cause of a failure. `E` is the error set it can fail with.
pub opaque type Cause[E](failure: fn() -> Never fails E)

## The two kinds of causes.
pub enum CauseKind {
  ## An error raised with `fail`. Shows up in `E`.
  Fail
  ## A defect (`[error.defect]`). Does not show up in the type.
  Die
}
  derive Eq, Show

## The kind of the cause.
@intrinsic
pub fn kind[E](c: Cause[E]) -> CauseKind

## The cause as text: `error: ...` or `defect: ...`.
@intrinsic
pub fn to_string[E](c: Cause[E]) -> String

## Raises the cause again: `Fail` fails with the error, and `Die` is a defect.
@intrinsic
pub fn raise[A, E](c: Cause[E]) -> A fails E
